  <h1>IVD MOQ and Lead Time</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> <strong>MOQ</strong> (minimum order quantity) is the smallest order a manufacturer will produce, set by packaging, cassette moulding and labelling economics. <strong>Lead time</strong> is the time from order confirmation to shipment — production plus, for OEM, a validation cycle. Both rise with customization.</p>

  <h2>What drives MOQ</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Format</strong> — cassettes, panels and cups have moulding and assembly minimums.</li>
    <li><strong>Packaging</strong> — printed boxes, pouches and private-label branding each add a minimum.</li>
    <li><strong>Customization</strong> — a custom cut-off or panel raises the minimum versus an off-the-shelf product.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What drives lead time</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>OEM validation</strong> — a new specification must be developed and re-validated before production.</li>
    <li><strong>Production</strong> — manufacturing, assembly, QC and lot release.</li>
    <li><strong>Shipping</strong> — sea or air, which trades cost against speed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to plan around them</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Choose <a href="/knowledge/buyer-oem-vs-odm/">ODM over OEM</a> when you need a shorter lead time.</li>
    <li>Confirm MOQ and lead time in writing before committing to a spec.</li>
    <li>Build validation time into your launch plan — OEM is not instant.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
  <p><strong>Position:</strong> In my view, the most common MOQ/lead-time mistake is a buyer locking a specification without confirming the minimum and the validation cycle — then being surprised that a "small first order" is not worth producing.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> MOQ and lead time are functions of customization, not arbitrary. If your order is small, buy an existing (ODM) product; if your spec is unique, expect a higher MOQ and a longer lead time to cover the validation. Plan the numbers with the supplier before you plan the launch.</p>
  <p><strong>Disclosure:</strong> This is my editorial view as an IVD manufacturer's technical team, not a quote or contractual statement.</p>

